WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:01.668
-So happy to have you back.
-Thank you. Glad to be back.

00:00:01.668 --> 00:00:03.203
-It was very exciting for us
the last time you were here,

00:00:03.203 --> 00:00:05.705 align:start size:94% position:6%
which is the first time
you were here.

00:00:05.705 --> 00:00:06.373 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
We, after the show,

00:00:06.373 --> 00:00:08.875 align:start size:94% position:6%
we have a party for our show
every summer.

00:00:08.875 --> 00:00:10.143 line:92%
And you were a guest that night,

00:00:10.143 --> 00:00:11.544 align:start size:94% position:6%
and you came
to our summer party.

00:00:11.544 --> 00:00:14.080 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it was so exciting
for the "Late Night" staff

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:15.582 align:start size:94% position:6%
to have you there.
-I gotta tell you,

00:00:15.582 --> 00:00:18.018
being a kid from New York City,
I mean, from Brooklyn,

00:00:18.018 --> 00:00:21.054
and my aunt, God bless her soul,
she died of --

00:00:21.054 --> 00:00:23.089 align:start size:94% position:6%
She used to always take me
to the Rockettes.

00:00:23.089 --> 00:00:24.124 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, Easter --
the Easter show

00:00:24.124 --> 00:00:26.726 align:start size:94% position:6%
and the Christmas show.
So I always came to this area.

00:00:26.726 --> 00:00:29.262
And -- But I never got a chance
to go to the roof

00:00:29.262 --> 00:00:31.931 align:start size:94% position:6%
of the building to
see that view of the city.

00:00:31.931 --> 00:00:33.099 align:start size:94% position:6%
So I got up there,
and it was just, like,

00:00:33.099 --> 00:00:35.135 align:start size:94% position:6%
my face was glued to
the glass like,

00:00:35.135 --> 00:00:37.837 align:start size:94% position:6%
"I'm at the top of the world!"
[ Laughter ]

00:00:37.837 --> 00:00:40.573 align:start size:94% position:6%
Like, I guess I should go
and mingle and say something.

00:00:40.573 --> 00:00:41.775 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Well, it was nice.
Thank you for mingling.

00:00:41.775 --> 00:00:44.277
And I just want you to know, it
was a huge step down this year

00:00:44.277 --> 00:00:45.545 align:start size:94% position:6%
with no Michael K. Williams.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:45.545 --> 00:00:47.881 align:start size:94% position:6%
So we're gonna line you up
this year in advance.

00:00:47.881 --> 00:00:49.182 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I'll be there.
Put in my sked.

00:00:49.182 --> 00:00:51.618
-Now we talked,
when you were here, about Omar,

00:00:51.618 --> 00:00:54.554 align:start size:94% position:6%
one of the great characters of
all time on "The Wire."

00:00:54.554 --> 00:00:57.357 align:start size:94% position:6%
Just truly...
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:57.357 --> 00:01:00.493 align:start size:94% position:6%
I don't know it you saw this,
but, uh, this was --

00:01:00.493 --> 00:01:04.364
People put this online about
the Mueller investigation.

00:01:04.364 --> 00:01:05.365 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Uh-Oh.
-It's sort of a meme

00:01:05.365 --> 00:01:08.168
that's been floating around
called "Mueller Coming."

00:01:08.168 --> 00:01:11.771 align:start size:94% position:6%
And this is what...
[ Laughter ]

00:01:11.771 --> 00:01:14.607 align:start size:94% position:6%
Mueller on Omar's head,
which makes sense

00:01:14.607 --> 00:01:16.609 align:start size:94% position:6%
'cause it does feel like
Mueller's coming.

00:01:16.609 --> 00:01:17.911 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
-The difference is that,

00:01:17.911 --> 00:01:19.512 align:start size:94% position:6%
I feel like people hid
from Omar,

00:01:19.512 --> 00:01:21.948 align:start size:94% position:6%
and everybody in the Trump
administration is like,

00:01:21.948 --> 00:01:23.983 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Hey, we're over here!"
-"Over here!"

00:01:23.983 --> 00:01:24.818 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:01:24.818 --> 00:01:28.254 align:start size:94% position:6%
"We're doing dumb stuff
over here!"

00:01:28.254 --> 00:01:31.624 align:start size:94% position:6%
So Omar, again,
such a crazy character.

00:01:31.624 --> 00:01:33.626 line:92%
But you played it in a way

00:01:33.626 --> 00:01:35.695 align:start size:94% position:6%
that the writers started
challenging you as an actor.

00:01:35.695 --> 00:01:38.898 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I guess this wasn't
originally in the DNA of Omar,

00:01:38.898 --> 00:01:43.536 align:start size:94% position:6%
but you were given Briticisms,
things that were not true

00:01:43.536 --> 00:01:46.306 align:start size:94% position:6%
to what somebody, maybe,
in Baltimore would talk like.

00:01:46.306 --> 00:01:47.907 line:92%
-You know, now that I realize

00:01:47.907 --> 00:01:50.376 align:start size:94% position:6%
that I was like, you know,
"Nice going, Dave."

00:01:50.376 --> 00:01:52.979
But, you know, we were kids
when we came to that show.

00:01:52.979 --> 00:01:55.582 line:92%
And I know for myself, I was --

00:01:55.582 --> 00:01:59.119 align:start size:94% position:6%
Didn't have a lot of success
academically speaking.

00:01:59.119 --> 00:02:01.521
You know, didn't excel in school
and all that stuff.

00:02:01.521 --> 00:02:04.257
So, you know, it was like
a learning experience.

00:02:04.257 --> 00:02:06.759 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, I came to the show
very green

00:02:06.759 --> 00:02:07.927 align:start size:94% position:6%
and very immature
in a lot of ways.

00:02:07.927 --> 00:02:10.029 align:start size:94% position:6%
And, like, David,
like, if you give a kid

00:02:10.029 --> 00:02:12.966
opportunity to excel, they'll
probably rise to the occasion.

00:02:12.966 --> 00:02:14.767 align:start size:94% position:6%
So it was kind of like that.
And where else would

00:02:14.767 --> 00:02:18.171 align:start size:94% position:6%
I get a chance to, you know,
to make "Do tell" work

00:02:18.171 --> 00:02:20.140 align:start size:94% position:6%
in a sentence
than on "The Wire," right?

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:21.207 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.

00:02:21.207 --> 00:02:24.611 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Quote Greek mythology and
really have it sound gangster.

00:02:24.611 --> 00:02:25.745 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
-Only "The Wire."

00:02:25.745 --> 00:02:27.147 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Only "The Wire."
Yeah, that's true.

00:02:27.147 --> 00:02:28.982 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Do tell" was not something...
-No.

00:02:28.982 --> 00:02:30.316 align:start size:94% position:6%
-...that I feel like was
being said a lot

00:02:30.316 --> 00:02:31.551 align:start size:94% position:6%
on the streets of Baltimore.
-No.

00:02:31.551 --> 00:02:32.785 align:start size:94% position:6%
-But Omar said it.
-We made it work, right?

00:02:32.785 --> 00:02:34.154 size:81% position:41%
-You did make it work.
You did make it work.

00:02:34.154 --> 00:02:36.723
In "Hap and Leonard,"
you play Leonard, which is also

00:02:36.723 --> 00:02:39.425
a very -- a character with
a lot of contradictions.

00:02:39.425 --> 00:02:43.429 align:start size:94% position:6%
You are a black, Republican,
gay, veteran.

00:02:43.429 --> 00:02:45.431
Is it great when you --
-Who likes country music.

00:02:45.431 --> 00:02:47.066
-Who likes country music. Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:47.066 --> 00:02:48.535 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah, don't forget that part.
-Was that --

00:02:48.535 --> 00:02:50.937
Is that an exciting thing,
when you get into a script

00:02:50.937 --> 00:02:52.906 align:start size:94% position:6%
and you realize all these
different elements

00:02:52.906 --> 00:02:54.307 align:start size:94% position:6%
of a character
that you get to play?

00:02:54.307 --> 00:02:56.509 align:start size:94% position:6%
-It's very -- it's very --
it's -- it's exciting.

00:02:56.509 --> 00:02:58.878 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's intimidating.
It's challenging.

00:02:58.878 --> 00:03:00.113 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, it's -- it's --
you know,

00:03:00.113 --> 00:03:02.582 align:start size:94% position:6%
it's out of the comfort zone
of what, you know,

00:03:02.582 --> 00:03:04.350 line:92%
we may consider to be normal.

00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:07.487 align:start size:94% position:6%
And, you know, who --
which actor doesn't like

00:03:07.487 --> 00:03:09.055 align:start size:94% position:6%
those types of opportunities,
right?

00:03:09.055 --> 00:03:11.624 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
-But, um, it was -- He's a --

00:03:11.624 --> 00:03:13.826 align:start size:94% position:6%
Leonard is a bunch
of walking contradictions

00:03:13.826 --> 00:03:16.963 align:start size:94% position:6%
and he goes against the grain.
And, you know, I love --

00:03:16.963 --> 00:03:20.667
But mostly what I loved about
this show is the friendship.

00:03:20.667 --> 00:03:23.970 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think that Hap and Leonard,
they define

00:03:23.970 --> 00:03:26.472 align:start size:94% position:6%
what male friendship should
look like.

00:03:26.472 --> 00:03:28.875 align:start size:94% position:6%
It shouldn't be
about race or gender.

00:03:28.875 --> 00:03:30.910 align:start size:94% position:6%
If two men love each other
and consider themselves

00:03:30.910 --> 00:03:34.013
to be brothers and friends
and they accept each other,

00:03:34.013 --> 00:03:35.949 size:81% position:41%
we shouldn't be judged
for that friendship.

00:03:35.949 --> 00:03:39.519
And I think that that's the main
thing I love to explore,

00:03:39.519 --> 00:03:41.988 line:92%
is reintroducing or exploring

00:03:41.988 --> 00:03:44.524 align:start size:94% position:6%
what male friendship is
in America

00:03:44.524 --> 00:03:45.925 line:92%
and what that really looks like.

00:03:45.925 --> 00:03:47.827 align:start size:94% position:6%
-You obviously have
a great partner in this,

00:03:47.827 --> 00:03:51.397 align:start size:94% position:6%
which is -- Absolutely.
[ Applause ]

